// UAP Report Atlas — case data
// Compiled from AARO's Historical Record Report Vol. I, AARO FY22–FY25 annual
// reports to Congress, AARO individual case-resolution reports, congressional
// UAP hearing testimony (2023–2025), and corroborating news coverage.
//
// STATUS values mirror AARO's own classification language:
//   unresolved      — reviewed, no explanation reached
//   explained       — AARO/USG reached a prosaic explanation (balloon, drone, satellite, etc.)
//   uncorroborated  — insufficient evidence to assess either way
//
// PRECISION: "exact" = a named, specific site; "approx" = illustrative placement
// within a named region because no precise site was publicly disclosed.
//
// IMAGE HONESTY POLICY — imageType is one of:
//   "photo"       — a genuine photograph. The caption states plainly whether it
//                   shows the actual object/event or is a representative photo of
//                   the location, aircraft type, or object class involved.
//   "video-still" — a frame captured from officially released government footage,
//                   not a standalone photograph.
//   "illustration"— an artist's depiction, official government rendering, replica,
//                   or diagram. NOT a photograph, and always labeled as such.
//   null          — no verifiable image exists for this case; none is shown.
// No image here is presented as evidence of anything anomalous.

const CASES = [
  { id:"tictac", name:"“Tic Tac” / FLIR1", date:"2004-11-14", lat:32.75, lon:-118.05, location:"Pacific Ocean, ~100mi SW of San Diego, CA", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"On November 14, 2004, F/A-18F pilots from the USS Nimitz carrier strike group — including Cmdr. David Fravor and Lt. Cmdr. Alex Dietrich — were vectored to investigate radar returns and visually observed a smooth, white, oblong object roughly 40 feet long maneuvering erratically over the Pacific. Lt. Cmdr. Chad Underwood later recorded the object on his jet's ATFLIR targeting sensor, producing the infrared clip known as “FLIR1.” The footage circulated privately for years before the Pentagon's AATIP program released it in 2017, and the Department of Defense formally confirmed its authenticity in April 2020. The object's rapid, seemingly inertia-defying movements remain officially unexplained, and the case is frequently cited as a founding incident of the modern U.S. government UAP inquiry.", source:"U.S. Navy (2019 authentication); AARO", image:"images/tictac.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from the officially released FLIR1 infrared targeting-pod footage showing the elongated object tracked by Navy sensors.", imageCredit:"U.S. Department of Defense / U.S. Navy, released April 2020 (public domain)" },

  { id:"gimbal", name:"“Gimbal”", date:"2015", lat:30.3, lon:-79.9, location:"Atlantic Ocean, off the U.S. East Coast", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"Recorded in January 2015 by an F/A-18 aircrew from the USS Theodore Roosevelt strike group, the Gimbal footage shows a dark, disc-like object that appears to rotate or tumble while moving against strong high-altitude winds. On the audio track a crewman can be heard exclaiming “Look at that thing, dude!” and noting that it is rotating. Some analysts argue the apparent rotation is an optical artifact caused by the targeting pod's gimbal mechanism re-orienting as it tracks, combined with infrared glare, rather than genuine rotation of the object. Like FLIR1 and GoFast, the clip leaked publicly around 2017 before the DoD officially released and authenticated it in April 2020.", source:"U.S. Navy (2019 authentication); AARO", image:"images/gimbal.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from the officially released “Gimbal” infrared cockpit-sensor footage showing the object against a cloud layer.", imageCredit:"U.S. Department of Defense / U.S. Navy, released April 2020 (public domain)" },

  { id:"gofast", name:"“GoFast”", date:"2015", lat:26.9, lon:-80.3, location:"Atlantic Ocean, off Florida", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"Recorded by a Navy F/A-18 aircrew from the USS Theodore Roosevelt in 2015, the GoFast video appears to show a small, wingless object skimming just above the ocean at extreme apparent speed as the infrared pod locks onto it. AARO's technical review concluded the dramatic apparent velocity was largely a parallax illusion: because the sensor looked down at a steep angle from a fast, high-altitude aircraft, a comparatively slow object appeared to race across the water below. AARO assessed the object's true airspeed as consistent with wind-driven drift rather than propelled flight. The clip was one of three Navy videos officially released and authenticated by the Pentagon in April 2020.", source:"AARO technical review, 2023", image:"images/gofast.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from the officially released “GoFast” infrared footage showing the small dark object low over the ocean.", imageCredit:"U.S. Department of Defense / U.S. Navy, released April 2020 (public domain)" },

  { id:"aguadilla", name:"Aguadilla Encounter", date:"2013-04-25", lat:18.43, lon:-67.15, location:"Aguadilla, Puerto Rico", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"DHS / Customs and Border Protection", summary:"A CBP Dash-8 patrol aircraft equipped with a Wescam electro-optical/infrared turret recorded roughly three minutes of footage near Rafael Hernández Airport, showing one or more dark objects moving erratically at low altitude and appearing to dive toward and emerge from the ocean surface. The video was not officially released at the time but was later obtained and analyzed by outside researchers, including a multi-year study by the Scientific Coalition for UAP Studies that argued the objects showed anomalous flight characteristics. AARO subsequently assessed the case as most likely explainable by a combination of parallax, out-of-focus artifacts, and slow-moving or stationary balloons rather than objects entering the water. Critics of that conclusion note it does not fully account for the apparent water entry and the apparent multiplication of objects in the raw footage.", source:"AARO Case Resolution Report", image:"images/aguadilla.png", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Zoomed detail from the CBP infrared surveillance footage of the encounter. The circle is an annotation added by researchers for analysis, not part of the original video.", imageCredit:"U.S. Customs and Border Protection sensor footage (public domain); annotation added by analysts" },

  { id:"malmstrom", name:"Echo Flight Shutdown", date:"1967-03-16", lat:47.51, lon:-111.19, location:"Malmstrom AFB, Montana", precision:"exact", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Air Force / Strategic Air Command", summary:"On the morning of March 16, 1967, missile crew commander Capt. Eric Carlson and Lt. Robert Salas were on duty in the underground Echo Flight launch control center when topside security guards reported a glowing, reddish-orange object hovering near the perimeter fence. Within roughly the same window, all ten Minuteman I ICBMs under Echo Flight's control dropped into “no-go” status in rapid succession — a malfunction pattern Boeing engineers who inspected the missiles afterward could not attribute to any known guidance or wiring fault. A nearly identical shutdown at Oscar Flight occurred eight days later, also reportedly coinciding with a sighting. A later Pentagon review attributed the shutdowns to a classified electromagnetic-pulse test of the period, an explanation Salas and other witnesses have publicly disputed.", source:"Declassified USAF records; R. Salas testimony", image:"images/malmstrom.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"Interior of a preserved Minuteman launch control capsule of the same design and era. This is a representative photo of the facility type — not a photo of the 1967 incident, and no photograph of any object from that event is known to exist.", imageCredit:"Ronald Reagan Minuteman Missile State Historic Site, via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"rendlesham", name:"Rendlesham Forest Incident", date:"1980-12-26", lat:52.09, lon:1.44, location:"Rendlesham Forest, Suffolk, England", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Air Force (RAF Woodbridge)", summary:"Beginning the night of December 26, 1980, USAF security personnel from RAF Woodbridge — including Airmen Jim Penniston and John Burroughs — entered Rendlesham Forest after seeing unusual lights and reported encountering a triangular craft with strange markings among the trees. Deputy base commander Lt. Col. Charles Halt investigated further lights on subsequent nights and recorded his observations on a since-declassified audio tape. Investigators later examined three small triangular ground indentations, elevated radiation readings, and scorch marks on nearby trees, though skeptics have attributed these to old rabbit diggings and a rotting stump. The UK Ministry of Defence's own investigation, released under FOI, concluded there was no evidence of a threat to national security and drew no firm conclusion about the objects' nature.", source:"Halt memo and audio tape; UK MoD file (released under FOI)", image:"images/rendlesham.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"The wooded clearing identified as the approximate site of the December 1980 reports, photographed decades later. This shows the location only — no photograph of any craft from the incident is known to exist.", imageCredit:"Simon Leatherdale, via Geograph Britain and Ireland / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0)" },

  { id:"lakehuron", name:"Lake Huron Intercept", date:"2023-02-12", lat:44.4, lon:-82.4, location:"Over Lake Huron, near the U.S.–Canada border", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Air National Guard / NORAD", summary:"On February 12, 2023, an Air National Guard F-16 shot down an unidentified octagonal object with strings or cables dangling beneath it over Lake Huron — three days after similar shootdowns of a Chinese surveillance balloon off South Carolina and an unidentified object over Alaska. Pilots who intercepted it said they could not positively identify the object, with one remarking he “wouldn't really call it a balloon.” Debris was never recovered from the lake despite a search. Cockpit infrared footage was later declassified under the PURSUE declassification program, after which AARO assessed the imagery as consistent with a benign hobbyist or research balloon trailing tangled cabling, though the object's owner and origin were never conclusively identified.", source:"NORAD; declassified cockpit footage (PURSUE release)", image:"images/lakehuron.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from declassified F-16 infrared cockpit footage showing the object shortly before the intercept.", imageCredit:"U.S. Department of War, PURSUE declassification release (public domain)" },

  { id:"fortbelvoir", name:"Fort Belvoir Ring Object", date:"1957-09", lat:38.7126, lon:-77.1466, location:"Fort Belvoir, Virginia", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"U.S. Army", summary:"One morning in September 1957, an Army private at Fort Belvoir was called outside by fellow soldiers to see a solid-looking, non-reflective black ring gliding across the sky; he retrieved a Brownie camera and took six photographs before the ring became enveloped in white smoke roughly five minutes later. The photographs were not submitted to investigators until 1966, when they reached NICAP and drew the interest of physicist Dr. James E. McDonald. The University of Colorado's Condon Report devoted eleven pages (Case 50) to analyzing the images, ultimately concluding the ring was almost certainly a smoke or vortex ring produced by a simulated atomic-bomb demonstration — a training exercise using drums of fuel and explosives that Fort Belvoir is documented to have conducted in this period. Because the original negatives were lost, analysis relied on surviving prints.", source:"Condon Report, Case 50; NICAP archives", image:"images/fortbelvoir.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"One of the six photographs taken by the soldier, showing the dark ring-shaped object. This is a genuine photograph of the reported object, later attributed to a smoke ring from a training exercise.", imageCredit:"Photographer identified in case files; reproduced in the 1968 Condon Report (Case 50)" },

  { id:"socorro", name:"Socorro / White Sands Landing", date:"1964-04-24", lat:34.0584, lon:-106.8914, location:"Socorro, NM, adjacent to White Sands Missile Range", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Army (White Sands) / USAF Project Blue Book", summary:"On the evening of April 24, 1964, Socorro police officer Lonnie Zamora, while chasing a speeding car, heard a roar and saw a flame descending into a gully; investigating, he found a smooth, whitish, egg-shaped object resting on legs with two small figures in white coveralls beside it, and a red insignia on its side. As he approached, the object emitted a loud roar and a blue-orange flame and ascended rapidly. Sgt. Sam Chavez arrived shortly after to find four rectangular landing-leg impressions and patches of burning brush. Project Blue Book's scientific consultant J. Allen Hynek personally investigated and found Zamora credible; Blue Book ultimately listed the case as “unidentified,” one of very few never assigned a conventional explanation.", source:"Project Blue Book; Army investigation notes", image:"images/socorro.jpg", imageType:"illustration", imageCaption:"Modern digital reconstruction of the craft and insignia based on Officer Zamora's sketches and testimony. This is an illustration, not a photograph — no photo of the object was ever taken.", imageCredit:"3D-modeled illustration by Wikimedia Commons contributor “JMK” (CC BY-SA 3.0)" },

  { id:"la1942", name:"Battle of Los Angeles", date:"1942-02-24", lat:33.94, lon:-118.41, location:"Los Angeles, California", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"U.S. Army / Army Air Forces", summary:"In the early hours of February 25, 1942, less than three months after Pearl Harbor, coastal defense units around Los Angeles opened fire on an unidentified aerial target, expending over 1,400 rounds of anti-aircraft ammunition across several hours. A widely reproduced Los Angeles Times photograph appeared to show searchlight beams converging on a disc-like shape; research has since established the image was heavily retouched in the newspaper's photo department — standard practice at the time to boost newsprint contrast — and that the “disc” was a blur of flak bursts and light. A 1983 Office of Air Force History review concluded the panic was likely triggered by a stray weather balloon combined with wartime nerves. No wreckage or enemy aircraft was ever found, though several deaths were attributed to the ensuing chaos.", source:"Office of Air Force History review (1983)", image:"images/la1942.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"The Los Angeles Times photograph of searchlights over the city, published February 26, 1942. Note: this image was heavily retouched by the newspaper before publication, a fact established by later analysis.", imageCredit:"Los Angeles Times, Feb. 26, 1942, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain in the U.S.)" },

  { id:"mantell", name:"Mantell UFO Incident", date:"1948-01-07", lat:36.87, lon:-86.57, location:"Near Franklin, Kentucky (Fort Knox area)", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"USAF / Kentucky Air National Guard", summary:"On January 7, 1948, Kentucky Air National Guard Captain Thomas F. Mantell died when his F-51D Mustang crashed after he pursued an unidentified object at high altitude without supplemental oxygen, likely losing consciousness before impact. Ground observers, including state police and Godman Field tower personnel, reported a large metallic object, which Mantell described over radio as “metallic and of tremendous size.” Project Blue Book's later and now generally accepted explanation is that Mantell was chasing a classified Navy Skyhook balloon — part of a secret upper-atmosphere program not yet disclosed to Air Force or state investigators. Skyhook balloons were up to 100 feet across and highly reflective, consistent with witness descriptions, and several are documented to have launched that day.", source:"Project Blue Book", image:"images/mantell.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A U.S. Navy Skyhook balloon being launched in 1953 — the classified balloon type later blamed for the crash. This is a representative photo of that balloon type, not the specific balloon involved.", imageCredit:"U.S. Navy photograph 80-G-627386 (public domain)" },

  { id:"chileswhitted", name:"Chiles–Whitted UFO Encounter", date:"1948-07-24", lat:32.38, lon:-86.30, location:"Near Montgomery, Alabama", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"Eastern Air Lines / USAF Project Sign", summary:"At approximately 2:45 a.m. on July 24, 1948, Eastern Air Lines pilots Clarence Chiles and John Whitted, flying a DC-3 near Montgomery, reported a near-collision with a wingless, cigar-shaped craft roughly 100 feet long with a double row of glowing windows and a jet-like exhaust trail, which passed close by and shot upward into clouds. One passenger reported seeing only a brief streak of light, and a ground observer near Robins AFB in Georgia reported an unusual light in the same general timeframe. The two pilots each independently sketched the object days later and produced strikingly similar drawings. Project Blue Book's official conclusion attributed the sighting to a bright meteor fireball, though the reported duration, maneuvering, and structural detail described by two experienced airline pilots have kept the case a frequently cited classic.", source:"Project Sign case file; pilot accounts", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"gorman", name:"Gorman Dogfight", date:"1948-10-01", lat:46.88, lon:-96.79, location:"Fargo, North Dakota", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"North Dakota Air National Guard / USAF", summary:"On the night of October 1, 1948, North Dakota Air National Guard Lieutenant George F. Gorman, flying an F-51 Mustang near Fargo, spotted a round, sharply-edged, brilliantly lit object and requested tower permission to intercept it. What followed was a roughly 27-minute pursuit in which the object allegedly outmaneuvered his fighter repeatedly, at one point closing head-on before breaking away, then climbing away at high speed. Two independent ground witnesses, including another pilot and an air traffic controller, corroborated seeing maneuvering lights that night. The Air Force investigation attributed the sighting to a lighted weather balloon or possibly the planet Jupiter — though Gorman maintained for the rest of his life that he did not believe he had spent nearly half an hour dogfighting a balloon.", source:"Project Sign / Blue Book", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"lubbock", name:"Lubbock Lights", date:"1951-08-25", lat:33.58, lon:-101.85, location:"Lubbock, Texas", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF Project Grudge / Blue Book", summary:"Beginning August 25, 1951, a group of Texas Technological College professors observed a crescent-shaped formation of blue-white lights crossing the night sky over Lubbock, an event that repeated on subsequent nights and drew national press attention. On the night of August 30–31, Texas Tech freshman Carl Hart Jr. photographed the phenomenon from his backyard with a 35mm camera, capturing five images that were later reprinted in Life magazine. Project Blue Book investigators, including astronomer J. Allen Hynek, examined the photographs at length and could not conclusively debunk them. A widely cited explanation attributes both the visual sightings and the photos to migratory birds reflecting Lubbock's recently installed mercury-vapor streetlights, though the professors' original sighting and Hart's photographs are generally treated as only loosely related strands of the same flap.", source:"Project Blue Book", image:"images/lubbock.png", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"One of Carl Hart Jr.'s photographs of the light formation, taken August 30–31, 1951. A genuine period photograph; the lights' origin remains disputed.", imageCredit:"Carl Hart Jr., 1951 — low-resolution reproduction used under fair use; not confirmed public domain" },

  { id:"dcflap", name:"1952 Washington, D.C. UFO Incident", date:"1952-07-19", lat:38.85, lon:-77.04, location:"Washington, D.C. (National Airport airspace)", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"USAF / Civil Aeronautics Administration", summary:"Over two consecutive weekends in July 1952, air traffic controllers at Washington National Airport and radar operators at nearby Andrews AFB tracked multiple unidentified, slow-moving radar returns inside restricted airspace near the White House and Capitol, some corroborated by visual sightings from pilots and tower personnel. F-94 interceptors were scrambled on both weekends, but pilots reported the objects evading them. The story ran on front pages nationwide and prompted a rare Pentagon press conference by Air Force Major General John Samford. The Air Force attributed the returns to a temperature inversion producing anomalous radar propagation, though the simultaneous visual sightings have kept the “Washington flap” among the most scrutinized radar-visual cases on record. Blue Book logged more reports in 1952 than in the four preceding years combined.", source:"USAF investigation; CIA Robertson Panel", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"rb47", name:"RB-47 UFO Encounter", date:"1957-07-17", lat:30.0, lon:-90.0, location:"Gulf Coast region (MS/LA/TX/OK, in-flight)", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF Strategic Air Command", summary:"On the morning of July 17, 1957, a USAF RB-47H reconnaissance aircraft carrying six officers and specialized electronic countermeasures equipment was on a training mission when its crew began detecting an unidentified signal source near Meridian, Mississippi. Over the next roughly 90 minutes and 700+ miles across four states, the object was tracked intermittently across three independent channels simultaneously: visually by the co-pilot as an intense light, electronically via the aircraft's ECM receivers, and by ground radar at Duncanville, Texas — reportedly matching course changes across all three at once. Physicist James E. McDonald investigated extensively in the late 1960s and argued it was among the most rigorously multi-instrumented cases on record. Blue Book's file remained officially “unidentified,” a rarity.", source:"USAF crew debrief; Project Blue Book", image:"images/rb47.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A Boeing RB-47H Stratojet reconnaissance aircraft of the type flown during the encounter. Representative aircraft photo — not the specific aircraft involved.", imageCredit:"U.S. Air Force photograph, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain)" },

  { id:"levelland", name:"Levelland UFO Case", date:"1957-11-02", lat:33.59, lon:-102.38, location:"Levelland, Texas", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"USAF Project Blue Book / local police", summary:"Beginning shortly before 11 p.m. on November 2, 1957, Levelland police received over a dozen calls within a few hours from separate motorists on rural roads, describing a brilliantly lit, egg- or torpedo-shaped object roughly 200 feet long that caused their engines and headlights to fail as it approached — with power restored once it departed. First reported by farmhand Pedro Saucedo, similar independent reports followed from other drivers, plus a Levelland patrolman and county officials who also witnessed lights. A Project Blue Book investigator spent roughly one day on-site days after the event and the Air Force attributed the sightings to ball lightning or St. Elmo's fire from an electrical storm — an explanation criticized by J. Allen Hynek and later investigators given clear-weather reports and the consistent vehicle ignition-failure pattern.", source:"Project Blue Book", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"trindade", name:"Trindade Island Photographs", date:"1958-01-16", lat:-20.51, lon:-29.32, location:"Trindade Island, Brazil (South Atlantic)", precision:"exact", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"Brazilian Navy", summary:"On January 16, 1958, civilian photographer Almiro Barauna, aboard the Brazilian Navy vessel Almirante Saldanha during International Geophysical Year research, captured a sequence of photographs of a dark, ring-shaped object flying over the island in daylight, reportedly witnessed by dozens of sailors and officers on deck. The Navy initially confiscated and examined the film before releasing it, and the images were published in February 1958 after Brazilian president Juscelino Kubitschek personally authorized their release — an early instance of a national government publicly releasing UFO photographic evidence. The photographs remain heavily disputed: Barauna developed the film privately rather than under direct Navy oversight as procedure required, and had a documented history of staging hoax UFO photos, though others cite the multiple corroborating crew witnesses.", source:"Brazilian Navy inquiry; O Globo (Feb. 1958)", image:"images/trindade.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"Reproduction of Barauna's photographs as published in the Brazilian press in February 1958. Genuine period photographs, but their authenticity is disputed — the photographer had a documented history of staged UFO images.", imageCredit:"O Globo newspaper, Feb. 26, 1958, via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"kecksburg", name:"Kecksburg UFO Incident", date:"1965-12-09", lat:40.17, lon:-79.46, location:"Kecksburg, Pennsylvania", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"USAF / NASA", summary:"On the evening of December 9, 1965, a brilliant fireball was witnessed streaking across the sky over six U.S. states and Ontario, with numerous reports converging on woods near Kecksburg, southeast of Pittsburgh. Local residents said they found a large, bronze, acorn-shaped object bearing markings resembling hieroglyphics, and reported that Army personnel cordoned off the site and removed something on a flatbed truck — though the military stated searchers found nothing. Scientific analyses, including a 1967 study in the Journal of the Royal Astronomical Society of Canada, concluded the fireball was most likely a meteor bolide; NASA later said 2005 findings pointed to Soviet satellite debris but that supporting records had been lost.", source:"USAF / NASA statements; JRASC (1967)", image:"images/kecksburg.jpg", imageType:"illustration", imageCaption:"A full-scale replica of the reported “space acorn,” built for a television series in 1990 and now displayed publicly in Kecksburg. This is a replica illustrating eyewitness descriptions — not the actual object, of which no photograph exists.", imageCredit:"Via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)" },

  { id:"minot1968", name:"Minot AFB UFO Incident", date:"1968-10-24", lat:48.42, lon:-101.35, location:"Minot AFB, North Dakota", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF / Strategic Air Command", summary:"On October 24, 1968, missile-site security teams at Minot AFB reported a glowing object near the Minuteman ICBM launch facilities they patrolled, prompting a B-52H bomber crew already airborne on a training sortie to investigate. The bomber's radar navigator obtained a return that paced the aircraft at close range and unusual speed, while the pilots visually observed a large lighted object; ground personnel and the aircrew corroborated each other's independent observations. Staff Sergeant Richard Clark, stationed at Minot, preserved a set of radarscope photographs that later surfaced as higher-quality prints than those in the Blue Book file. The case remains among the more frequently cited military radar-visual incidents due to the combination of ground, airborne, and radar corroboration.", source:"USAF/SAC incident report; Project Blue Book", image:"images/minot1968.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"Minot Air Force Base with a B-52 Stratofortress. Representative photo of the base and the aircraft type flown by the reporting crew — not a photo of the incident or any object.", imageCredit:"Via Wikimedia Commons (public domain, U.S. Air Force)" },

  { id:"coyne", name:"Coyne Helicopter Incident", date:"1973-10-18", lat:40.76, lon:-82.52, location:"Near Mansfield, Ohio (Charles Mill Lake)", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Army Reserve", summary:"On the night of October 18, 1973, an Army Reserve UH-1H helicopter crewed by Capt. Lawrence Coyne, 1st Lt. Arrigo Jezzi, Sgt. John Healey, and Sgt. Robert Yanacsek reported a near-collision near Mansfield with a cigar-shaped, gray metallic object bearing a dome, after which the helicopter reportedly rose sharply without engine power. Two crew members independently sketched the object afterward and produced strikingly similar drawings. The case was investigated extensively by Dr. J. Allen Hynek and researcher Jennie Zeidman for the newly formed Center for UFO Studies, becoming one of its founding landmark cases and the subject of Zeidman's 1979 monograph. Unusually for the era, Coyne reported the encounter to the media directly upon landing and later described it on national television.", source:"Center for UFO Studies; crew accounts", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"loring", name:"Loring AFB UFO Incursions", date:"1975-10-27", lat:46.95, lon:-67.88, location:"Loring AFB, Limestone, Maine", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF / Strategic Air Command", summary:"Beginning around October 27, 1975 and continuing intermittently for weeks, Loring AFB security personnel reported a low-flying, unidentified object repeatedly circling and approaching the base's nuclear Weapons Storage Area, prompting security alerts and aircraft scrambles. Loring was a Strategic Air Command installation home to B-52 bombers and one of the closest SAC bases to the Soviet Union during the Cold War, making its storage area a high-security nuclear facility. The incident is documented in declassified National Military Command Center memoranda and was one of a cluster of similar 1975 incursions at northern-tier SAC bases. The base closed in 1994 and the property was redeveloped as a commerce centre.", source:"Declassified NMCC memoranda", image:"images/loring.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A structure in the Weapons Storage Area at the former Loring AFB, photographed in 1998 for a federal historic documentation survey. Shows the facility type involved — not the incident itself.", imageCredit:"Historic American Engineering Record, Library of Congress (public domain)" },

  { id:"wurtsmith", name:"Wurtsmith AFB UFO Incursion", date:"1975-10-30", lat:44.45, lon:-83.39, location:"Wurtsmith AFB, Oscoda, Michigan", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF / Strategic Air Command", summary:"On October 30, 1975, security police at Wurtsmith AFB reported a hovering, lighted object over the base's weapons storage area; a KC-135 tanker crew vectored to investigate reported both visual sighting and radar contact before the object departed at high speed. Wurtsmith was a Strategic Air Command base hosting B-52 bombers and KC-135 tankers, and the incursion was one of a cluster of similar incidents reported that autumn at multiple northern-tier SAC bases including Loring, prompting internal Air Force review. The base closed in 1993 under base realignment; the land is now partly an industrial park and nature preserve.", source:"NORAD/SAC incident logs", image:"images/wurtsmith.png", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"The alert aircraft parking apron and tanker pens at Wurtsmith AFB. Representative photo of the high-security flightline infrastructure at the base — not the incident itself.", imageCredit:"U.S. Army Corps of Engineers historical documentation (public domain)" },

  { id:"tehran1976", name:"1976 Tehran UFO Incident", date:"1976-09-19", lat:35.69, lon:51.39, location:"Tehran, Iran", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"Imperial Iranian Air Force / U.S. DIA", summary:"In the early hours of September 19, 1976, civilians and military personnel in Tehran reported a bright, maneuvering object, prompting the Imperial Iranian Air Force to scramble two F-4 Phantom interceptors in succession. Both crews reported losing instrumentation, communications, and in one case weapons-system function as they closed on the object, with systems returning to normal once they broke off; one aircraft also reported a smaller object separating from the main one. The U.S. Defense Intelligence Agency documented the incident in a report later declassified via FOIA and circulated to the White House, CIA, NSA, and Air Force — part of why the case is regarded as among the more substantively documented military encounters on record.", source:"Declassified DIA report", image:"images/tehran1976.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"An Imperial Iranian Air Force F-4 Phantom II, the variant flown by the intercepting crews. Representative aircraft photo — not the specific jets involved or the object reported.", imageCredit:"Via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"jal1628", name:"Japan Air Lines Flight 1628 Incident", date:"1986-11-17", lat:64.0, lon:-145.0, location:"Airspace over eastern/central Alaska", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"Federal Aviation Administration", summary:"On November 17, 1986, Japan Air Lines Cargo Flight 1628, a Boeing 747-200F captained by Kenji Terauchi, reported being paced by lighted objects for roughly 30–50 minutes over Alaska, including two smaller objects and what Terauchi described as a larger “mothership.” Anchorage FAA radar registered intermittent unexplained returns near the aircraft's position, and the case drew attention because it combined an airline captain's account, a corroborating brief visual from a United pilot, and FAA radar data in one file. FAA division chief John Callahan later stated that a same-day briefing with military and intelligence personnel was unusually secretive. Analyses have suggested Terauchi may have misidentified Jupiter and Mars combined with lighting effects, though the case remains officially unresolved.", source:"FAA investigation report", image:"images/jal1628.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A JAL Cargo Boeing 747-200F of the same type and livery family used on Flight 1628. Representative aircraft photo — not from the incident.", imageCredit:"Via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"belgium", name:"Belgian UFO Wave / F-16 Intercept", date:"1990-03-30", lat:50.63, lon:6.03, location:"Eupen, Belgium", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"Belgian Air Force / Gendarmerie", summary:"Between November 1989 and early 1990, thousands of witnesses across Belgium reported large, silent, triangular craft with bright lights, with police and Gendarmerie officers among the observers. The wave's most significant event came on the night of March 30–31, 1990, when Belgian ground radar detected an unidentified target and two F-16s were scrambled from Beauvechain air base; the jets achieved several brief radar locks on an object reportedly executing extreme velocity and altitude changes before each lock was lost, though neither pilot achieved visual contact. The Belgian air force later released the intercept's radar data for independent analysis — a rare instance of a government publishing raw tracking data from a UFO intercept. Note that the wave's most famous photograph, the “Petit-Rechain” triangle image, was confessed as a hoax by its creator in 2011 and is deliberately not used here.", source:"Belgian Air Force / SOBEPS report", image:"images/belgium.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A Belgian Air Force F-16 Fighting Falcon, the aircraft type that carried out the March 1990 intercept. Representative aircraft photo — not from the incident.", imageCredit:"Via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"roswell", name:"Roswell Incident", date:"1947-07", lat:33.39, lon:-104.52, location:"Roswell, New Mexico", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"U.S. Army Air Forces", summary:"In July 1947, debris recovered from a ranch near Roswell prompted a brief Army Air Forces press release referring to a recovered “flying disc,” retracted within hours in favor of a weather balloon explanation — a sequence that fueled decades of speculation. AARO's Historical Record Report Vol. I traced the debris to a Project Mogul balloon train, a then-classified program using high-altitude balloon arrays with radar reflectors to detect Soviet nuclear tests, and found no evidence of alien bodies or a recovered craft. The foil-and-balsa reflector material recovered is consistent with the debris photographed at Fort Worth Army Air Field days later. AARO's review also found no evidence supporting later claims of a concealed recovery program. That said, the official account has itself shifted repeatedly — a weather balloon in 1947, Project Mogul in the Air Force's 1994 report, and anthropomorphic test dummies offered in its 1997 follow-up to explain reported “bodies,” despite those dummy drops not beginning until the 1950s. Renewed congressional attention to UAP since 2023, including whistleblower allegations of undisclosed crash-retrieval programs, has revived public claims that the Air Force concealed what was actually found. AARO examined those allegations and found no supporting evidence; critics counter that much of the primary 1947 documentation no longer survives, leaving the case publicly contested even as official reviews consider it closed.", source:"AARO Historical Record Report Vol. I; USAF reports (1994, 1997)", image:"images/roswell.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"Army Air Forces officers examining the recovered debris — foil, paper, and balsa struts — at Fort Worth Army Air Field, July 1947. A genuine period press photograph of the actual recovered material.", imageCredit:"1947 press photograph, U.S. Army Air Forces (public domain)" },

  { id:"arnold", name:"Kenneth Arnold Sighting", date:"1947-06-24", lat:46.85, lon:-121.76, location:"Mount Rainier area, Washington State", precision:"exact", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"Civilian pilot (reviewed by USG)", summary:"On June 24, 1947, private pilot Kenneth Arnold reported seeing nine crescent-shaped objects flying in formation near Mount Rainier at what he estimated to be extraordinary speed. His description of their motion — like “a saucer if you skipped it across water” — was condensed by the press into the phrase “flying saucer,” which entered global usage almost immediately. AARO's Historical Record Report cites the Arnold sighting as the origin point of the modern UFO reporting era, noting that the resulting media wave shaped how subsequent reports were described and categorized. Arnold himself later objected that the press had mischaracterized the objects' shape, which he described as crescent- or boomerang-like rather than disc-shaped.", source:"AARO Historical Record Report Vol. I", image:"images/arnold.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"Kenneth Arnold photographed with a camera beside his aircraft after the sighting. A genuine photograph of the witness — not of the objects he reported, which were never photographed.", imageCredit:"Period press photograph, via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"foofighters", name:"“Foo Fighter” Sightings", date:"1944", lat:49.0, lon:10.0, location:"European theater, WWII (regional)", precision:"approx", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Army Air Forces", summary:"During 1944–45, Allied aircrews in both the European and Pacific theaters repeatedly reported small, fast balls of light pacing their aircraft, often described as glowing orange or red spheres that maneuvered alongside bombers before departing. The phenomenon was nicknamed “foo fighters” by the 415th Night Fighter Squadron. Wartime investigations considered German secret weapons, static electricity, and misidentified aircraft, but no conclusion was reached, and no aircraft was ever reported damaged by one. AARO's Historical Record Report identifies foo fighters as the most high-profile UAP-type phenomenon reported by WWII aircrews, and notes their significance in establishing military aircrew reporting as a recurring source of anomalous accounts.", source:"AARO Historical Record Report Vol. I", image:"images/foofighters.png", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A grainy wartime aerial photograph showing an aircraft alongside two bright light-blobs, historically associated with foo fighter reports. Genuine period image; the lights' nature was never determined.", imageCredit:"WWII-era photograph, via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"greenfireballs", name:"Green Fireballs (Project Twinkle)", date:"1948-12", lat:35.88, lon:-106.30, location:"Los Alamos / Sandia / Kirtland region, New Mexico", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Army / Atomic Energy Commission", summary:"Beginning in December 1948, waves of bright green fireball sightings were reported over New Mexico, concentrated near Los Alamos, Sandia, and Kirtland — the heart of the U.S. nuclear weapons complex. Because the objects appeared to travel too flat and too slow for conventional meteors, and clustered around sensitive installations, the Air Force convened a 1949 scientific panel including Dr. Lincoln La Paz and Edward Teller, which reached no consensus explanation. This led to Project Twinkle, a dedicated instrumented observation effort using cameras and spectrographic equipment that ran from 1950 to 1951 but produced little conclusive data before being discontinued. AARO's historical review discusses the episode as an early example of anomalous phenomena reported near nuclear infrastructure.", source:"AARO Historical Record Report Vol. I; Project Twinkle records", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"aawsap", name:"AAWSAP/BAASS Utah Investigation", date:"2008", lat:40.25, lon:-109.89, location:"Private ranch property, Utah (approximate)", precision:"approx", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"DIA-funded contractor (BAASS)", summary:"From 2008 to 2010, the Defense Intelligence Agency funded the Advanced Aerospace Weapon System Applications Program, contracted to Bigelow Aerospace Advanced Space Studies, which among other activities investigated claims of anomalous and paranormal activity at a private ranch property in Utah's Uintah Basin. AARO's Historical Record Report reviewed the program and found the claims of anomalous entity activity at the site unsubstantiated, and further concluded that this line of investigation fell outside the program's legitimate scope and stated purpose. The review also addressed and rejected claims that the program had access to recovered non-human technology. The property remains privately owned and closed to the public.", source:"AARO Historical Record Report Vol. I", image:"images/aawsap.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"The gated entrance to the private Utah ranch property, posted with no-trespassing signage. Shows the site's public-facing perimeter only — no anomalous activity is depicted, and AARO found the claims about this property unsubstantiated.", imageCredit:"Via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"icbmclaims", name:"ICBM Site UAP Claims", date:"1966", lat:44.14, lon:-103.10, location:"Ellsworth AFB, South Dakota (related claims at Vandenberg AFB, CA and Minot AFB, ND)", precision:"approx", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Air Force", summary:"AARO's Historical Record Report describes interviews with five former U.S. Air Force personnel who claimed that UAP had disrupted power at ICBM launch control facilities during the 1960s and 1970s, and one who claimed to have seen video of a UAP destroying a test warhead in flight. AARO investigated each account, including reviewing relevant Air Force records and test footage archives, and found no corroborating evidence for any of the claims — noting in particular that the described test-warhead footage did not exist in any archive it could locate. The office assessed that some accounts appeared to conflate separate events or reflect secondhand information rather than direct observation.", source:"AARO Historical Record Report Vol. I", image:"images/icbmclaims.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"Interior of a Minuteman launch control capsule of the era described. Representative photo of the facility type referenced in these claims — not a photo of any incident, and AARO found no evidence supporting the accounts.", imageCredit:"Via Wikimedia Commons" },

  { id:"konablue", name:"“KONA BLUE” Program Claim", date:"2021", lat:38.91, lon:-77.02, location:"Washington, D.C. (Department of Homeland Security)", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"Department of Homeland Security", summary:"Whistleblower accounts alleged that a secret Department of Homeland Security program codenamed KONA BLUE had been established to house recovered non-human craft and biological material. AARO's Historical Record Report investigated and found that KONA BLUE was never an approved or operational program: it was a proposal, modeled on the earlier AAWSAP effort, that was pitched to DHS and formally rejected before any work began. AARO concluded that no such program ever existed as alleged and that no material of the kind described was ever held. The office characterized the episode as an example of how a rejected proposal can be misremembered or misrepresented downstream as evidence of an active concealment program.", source:"AARO Historical Record Report Vol. I", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"tremonton", name:"Tremonton, Utah UFO Film", date:"1952-07-02", lat:41.71, lon:-112.30, location:"Tremonton, Utah", precision:"exact", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"On July 2, 1952, Navy Chief Photographer Delbert Newhouse filmed roughly 75 seconds of color 16mm footage showing a cluster of bright objects moving against a clear sky near Tremonton, while traveling with his family. The film was analyzed at length by the Navy's Photographic Interpretation Center, which concluded the lights were not birds, balloons, or aircraft and estimated they were self-luminous. The Air Force's Project Blue Book and the later CIA-convened Robertson Panel disagreed, attributing the footage to sunlight reflecting off a flock of seagulls — a conclusion Newhouse rejected, noting he had observed the objects before beginning to film. The case is frequently paired with the Great Falls film as an early instance of formal government photographic analysis of anomalous footage.", source:"Naval Photographic Interpretation Center memo; Robertson Panel", image:"images/tremonton.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"A frame from Delbert Newhouse's 1952 color film showing the bright objects. A genuine frame from the original footage; the objects' nature remains disputed.", imageCredit:"U.S. Navy / Delbert Newhouse film (public domain)" },

  { id:"greatfalls", name:"Great Falls, Montana UFO Film", date:"1950-08-15", lat:47.50, lon:-111.30, location:"Great Falls, Montana", precision:"exact", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"On August 15, 1950, Nick Mariana, general manager of a local baseball team, filmed two bright objects moving across the sky over Great Falls using a 16mm camera. Mariana stated that the first frames — which he said showed the objects more clearly, including a rotating quality — were missing when the film was returned after Air Force analysis, a claim the Air Force disputed. Naval photographic analysts examined the footage alongside the Tremonton film and concluded the objects were not birds or balloons. Project Blue Book ultimately attributed the lights to reflections from two F-94 jets known to be in the area, though Mariana maintained the jets were visible separately in the sky at the time.", source:"Naval Photographic Interpretation Center memo; Project Blue Book", image:"images/greatfalls.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"A frame from Nick Mariana's 1950 film showing the two bright objects. A genuine frame from the original footage; officially attributed to aircraft reflections, which the filmer disputed.", imageCredit:"Nick Mariana film, analyzed by U.S. Navy / USAF (public domain)" },

  { id:"southeastasia", name:"Southeast Asia Triangles", date:"2017", lat:12.0, lon:114.0, location:"South China Sea (regional)", precision:"approx", status:"explained", agency:"Department of Defense / AARO", summary:"Surveillance imagery captured in 2017 showed triangle-shaped objects apparently floating over the water in Southeast Asian waters, which circulated for years as an unexplained UAP case. AARO's case-resolution analysis assessed the objects as almost certainly cone-shaped fishing nets — a common regional design that floats at the surface with a raised triangular profile — based on shape, scale, distribution, and comparison against known fishing equipment in the area. The office published the resolution in February 2024 as an example of an image that appears anomalous largely because of viewing angle and sensor resolution rather than the object itself. The case is often cited as a demonstration of how context and scale reference resolve seemingly anomalous imagery.", source:"AARO Case Resolution Report (Feb. 2024)", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"altaqaddum", name:"Al Taqaddum “Jellyfish” Object", date:"2017-10-23", lat:33.34, lon:43.57, location:"Al Taqaddum Air Base, Iraq", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"U.S. military aerostat sensor", summary:"On October 23, 2017, a U.S. military aerostat sensor at Al Taqaddum Air Base recorded roughly 17 minutes of infrared footage showing a glowing, jellyfish-like object with trailing appendages drifting over the base. The footage leaked publicly years later and circulated widely as an unexplained “transmedium” object. AARO's case-resolution analysis assessed with high confidence that the object was a cluster of fully and partially inflated balloons — the trailing “tendrils” being tether lines — noting the object's motion was consistent with prevailing winds throughout the recording. The office published its resolution in September 2025 alongside the underlying sensor data supporting the wind-drift correlation.", source:"AARO Case Resolution Report (Sept. 2025)", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"etna", name:"Mount Etna Object", date:"2018-12", lat:37.75, lon:15.00, location:"Mount Etna, Sicily, Italy", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"DoD / AARO (NAS Sigonella)", summary:"Short-wave infrared sensor video recorded in December 2018 appeared to show an object passing directly through Mount Etna's eruption plume, apparently unaffected by the volcanic column — an observation that circulated as evidence of anomalous durability. AARO's case-resolution analysis attributed the object to a wind-borne balloon, and the apparent “passing through unaffected” to atmospheric and optical distortion caused by the intense thermal gradient of the plume interacting with the infrared sensor. The office released its assessment in April 2025. The case is frequently cited by AARO as an example of how sensor artifacts near extreme heat sources can produce visually striking but prosaic results.", source:"AARO Case Resolution Report (Apr. 2025)", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"eglin", name:"Eglin UAP", date:"2023-01-26", lat:30.48, lon:-86.53, location:"Near Eglin Air Force Base, Florida", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"USAF pilot report", summary:"On January 26, 2023, a military pilot operating near Eglin Air Force Base reported a formation of four objects at approximately 16,000 feet, prompting an AARO review. Reconstructing the encounter geometry, wind data, and solar angle, AARO assessed with moderate confidence that the formation was a large commercial lighting balloon — a type of illuminated advertising or event balloon that can present as multiple discrete lights when viewed at distance and altitude. The case was published as one of AARO's individual case-resolution reports illustrating its analytic methodology. The same encounter is sometimes conflated with a separate Navy radar-malfunction report from the same period, which AARO assessed separately.", source:"AARO Case Resolution Report", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"harare", name:"Harare Airport Disc", date:"2008-07-02", lat:-17.93, lon:31.09, location:"Harare International Airport, Zimbabwe", precision:"exact", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"CIA (foreign reporting)", summary:"On July 2, 2008, a disc-shaped object with rotating lights on its underside was reported hovering over Harare International Airport in Zimbabwe, observed by airport personnel. The report prompted an internal CIA cable documenting the account and internal debate over the object's origin, including whether it might represent foreign aerospace activity. The cable was later declassified and released as part of the 2026 PURSUE declassification effort. No sensor data, photographs, or corroborating radar records accompanied the report, and the case remains uncorroborated — documented as a witness account transmitted through official channels rather than as an event with independent physical evidence.", source:"Declassified CIA cable (PURSUE release)", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"ussomaha", name:"USS Omaha Transmedium Encounter", date:"2019-07-15", lat:32.5, lon:-117.5, location:"Offshore San Diego, California", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"On July 15, 2019, combat information center personnel aboard the littoral combat ship USS Omaha tracked a spherical object off San Diego on radar and infrared sensors during a period of repeated unidentified contacts in Southern California operating areas. The infrared footage shows the object hovering and then descending toward the ocean surface, after which it disappeared from sensors; no wreckage, debris, or splash signature was recovered, and no submarine or surface asset accounted for it. Audio from the ship's watch team recording the encounter was included in the released material. The case is frequently cited in congressional testimony as an example of a multi-sensor Navy contact that remains formally unresolved.", source:"Navy incident report; congressional testimony", image:"images/ussomaha.png", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from the shipboard infrared footage showing the spherical object tracked by USS Omaha sensors.", imageCredit:"U.S. Navy sensor footage, released via official channels" },

  { id:"destroyerswarm", name:"2019 Navy Destroyer Drone Swarm", date:"2019-07", lat:33.9, lon:-119.5, location:"Off Southern California, near the Channel Islands", precision:"approx", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Navy (multiple destroyers)", summary:"Over several nights in July 2019, multiple U.S. Navy destroyers — including USS Kidd, USS Rafael Peralta, USS Russell, USS John Finn, and USS Paul Hamilton — reported formations of drone-like objects shadowing them off the Southern California coast, sometimes for hours and in low-visibility conditions. Ship logs, deck logs, and internal messages documenting the encounters were later obtained through Freedom of Information Act requests and confirmed as authentic by the Navy. Some crews described pyramid- or triangle-shaped objects hovering above the ships. Navy leadership stated the source of the objects was never identified; subsequent assessments have raised the possibility of foreign-operated or commercially available drones, but no attribution was ever confirmed.", source:"Navy deck logs (released under FOIA); Navy statements", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"cubeinsphere", name:"“Cube-in-Sphere” Virginia Beach Encounters", date:"2014", lat:36.85, lon:-75.5, location:"Warning Area W-72, off Virginia Beach, Virginia", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Navy (VFA-11)", summary:"Beginning around 2014, Navy F/A-18 pilots training in Warning Area W-72 off Virginia Beach reported repeated encounters with an object described as a dark cube contained inside a transparent sphere, encountered frequently enough that aircrews came to treat it as a known hazard. Lt. Ryan Graves testified to Congress in 2023 that one such object passed within roughly 50 feet of an aircraft, prompting a safety report. Graves emphasized that the objects appeared in the same operating areas over extended periods and that pilots were initially reluctant to report them due to stigma. The encounters contributed directly to the Navy's 2019 decision to formalize UAP reporting procedures for aircrews.", source:"Lt. Ryan Graves congressional testimony (2023)", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"trstrikegroup", name:"Theodore Roosevelt Strike Group Near-Midairs", date:"2015-01", lat:36.5, lon:-75.0, location:"Offshore U.S. East Coast (Virginia/North Carolina)", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"In January 2015, Navy leadership circulated an internal warning after multiple reported near-midair collisions between F/A-18 aircraft and unidentified objects during training operations off the East Coast, according to congressional testimony from Rear Adm. Tim Gallaudet. Gallaudet testified that he received an urgent internal Navy email describing the incidents and that the matter was treated as a flight-safety concern rather than an intelligence curiosity. The encounters overlapped in time and geography with the Gimbal and GoFast recordings from the same strike group. The episode is frequently cited as evidence that aircrew reports were being taken seriously internally years before public acknowledgment.", source:"Rear Adm. Tim Gallaudet congressional testimony", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"vandenberg", name:"Vandenberg AFB UAP Cluster", date:"2003", lat:34.73, lon:-120.57, location:"Vandenberg Air Force Base, California", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF Security Forces", summary:"Former Air Force security officer Jeffrey Nuccetelli testified to Congress about five distinct UAP incursions near missile-defense and launch facilities at Vandenberg Air Force Base between roughly 2003 and 2005, witnessed by multiple security personnel. One widely reported account involved a large red square-shaped object observed hovering over a launch facility, reported independently by more than one post. Nuccetelli testified that the reports were documented internally at the time but that personnel were discouraged from pursuing them further. No sensor data or imagery from the incidents has been publicly released, and the accounts rest on witness testimony rather than recorded evidence.", source:"J. Nuccetelli congressional testimony", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"elbosque", name:"El Bosque Chilean Navy FLIR UAP", date:"2014-11-11", lat:-33.0, lon:-71.6, location:"Coastal airspace between San Antonio and Quintero, Chile", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"Chilean Navy / CEFAA", summary:"On November 11, 2014, a Chilean Navy helicopter on a daytime patrol recorded roughly nine minutes of infrared footage of an elongated object that appeared to emit two plumes of thermal discharge, while flying a parallel course at similar altitude. Neither the aircrew nor ground radar at two nearby airports registered the object visually or on radar, despite clear conditions. Chile's Committee for the Study of Anomalous Aerial Phenomena (CEFAA) conducted a two-year investigation involving Navy, meteorological, and academic specialists, which considered and rejected explanations including aircraft, drones, and atmospheric effects. Some later independent analyses proposed a distant airliner with contrails as an explanation, though CEFAA's official finding classified the case as unidentified.", source:"Chilean Navy / CEFAA investigation", image:"images/elbosque.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from the Chilean Navy helicopter's infrared footage showing the elongated object and its thermal plumes.", imageCredit:"Chilean Navy / CEFAA, released publicly 2017" },

  { id:"ussjackson", name:"USS Jackson Tic-Tac Cluster", date:"2023", lat:21.0, lon:-158.0, location:"At sea, Pacific (vessel location approximate)", precision:"approx", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"Senior Chief Alexandro Wiggins, an active-duty sailor and the first serving service member to testify publicly on UAP, described observing four Tic-Tac-shaped objects from the USS Jackson that appeared to surface from the water, link up into a formation, and then depart with synchronized acceleration. Wiggins testified that the encounter was captured on the ship's sensor systems and observed by multiple crew members on watch. Infrared footage associated with the account was released publicly by an independent filmmaker rather than through official DoD channels, which limits independent verification of its provenance and processing. The case remains uncorroborated pending official confirmation of the underlying sensor data.", source:"Senior Chief A. Wiggins congressional testimony (2025)", image:"images/ussjackson.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Infrared frame associated with the reported encounter, showing an object in sensor crosshairs. Note: this footage was released through an independent filmmaker rather than official DoD channels, so its provenance is not officially confirmed.", imageCredit:"Released via independent journalist; original sensor source not officially confirmed" },

  { id:"chinaballoon", name:"Chinese High-Altitude Balloon", date:"2023-02-04", lat:33.7, lon:-78.9, location:"Off Myrtle Beach, South Carolina (shootdown site)", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"USAF / U.S. Navy", summary:"In late January 2023, a large high-altitude balloon assessed by U.S. officials as a People's Republic of China surveillance platform entered U.S. airspace over Alaska and traversed the continental United States over roughly a week, passing near sensitive military sites in Montana before being shot down by an F-22 off the South Carolina coast on February 4. Debris was recovered from shallow water and analyzed, with officials stating the payload carried intelligence-collection equipment; China maintained it was a civilian meteorological airship blown off course. The incident triggered heightened scrutiny of North American airspace that directly preceded the three unidentified-object shootdowns over Alaska, the Yukon, and Lake Huron the following week.", source:"DoD statements; NORAD", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"alaskaobj", name:"Alaska High-Altitude Object Shootdown", date:"2023-02-10", lat:70.0, lon:-149.0, location:"Beaufort Sea, off northern Alaska", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF / NORAD", summary:"On February 10, 2023, an F-22 downed a small, car-sized object flying at roughly 40,000 feet over sea ice near the Beaufort Sea after it was assessed as a hazard to civil aviation. F-35s from Eielson AFB and F-22s from Joint Base Elmendorf-Richardson conducted visual inspection passes before the engagement; pilots reported conflicting impressions of the object's appearance and could not identify any propulsion or payload. Recovery efforts on the ice were hampered by weather and the object was never retrieved, leaving its origin and purpose formally undetermined. No photograph of the object itself was ever publicly released.", source:"NORAD; White House and DoD briefings", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"yukonobj", name:"Yukon High-Altitude Object Shootdown", date:"2023-02-11", lat:63.0, lon:-136.0, location:"Yukon Territory, Canada", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"NORAD / USAF", summary:"On February 11, 2023, a U.S. F-22 operating under NORAD shot down a cylindrical object over remote Yukon Territory at the Canadian government's request, one day after the Alaska engagement. Debris was never recovered despite search efforts, hampered by deep snow and rugged terrain. In September 2024, a single low-resolution image of the object was released to CTV News under a freedom-of-information request; internal documents released alongside it showed the image had been cleared for public distribution within days of the incident, then withheld after a public affairs official expressed concern it “may create more questions/confusion.” Officials described the object at the time as a suspected balloon.", source:"NORAD; Government of Canada records (released under access-to-information)", image:"images/yukonobj.webp", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"The low-resolution image of the object released under a Canadian freedom-of-information request in 2024, showing a roughly doughnut-like shape. Officials described the object as a suspected balloon; the image quality precludes definitive identification.", imageCredit:"Government of Canada, released to CTV News under access-to-information request" },

  { id:"deirezzor", name:"Deir ez-Zor Metallic Orb", date:"2022-07-12", lat:35.34, lon:40.14, location:"Deir ez-Zor, Syria", precision:"exact", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"USAF (MQ-9 Reaper)", summary:"On July 12, 2022, an MQ-9 Reaper drone operating over Deir ez-Zor recorded a small, metallic sphere with no visible means of propulsion, control surfaces, or thermal signature, moving steadily across the frame. AARO's then-director presented the footage to the Senate Armed Services Committee in April 2023 as representative of roughly 650 similarly-shaped reports in the office's database, describing the metallic orb as the single most common UAP morphology reported. The location was later independently geolocated by open-source analysts. The case remains uncorroborated: AARO has stated that objects of this type typically lack sufficient sensor data for resolution, and no explanation has been assigned.", source:"AARO briefing to Senate Armed Services Committee, April 2023", image:"images/deirezzor.webp", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from the MQ-9 Reaper sensor footage showing the metallic sphere, as presented to Congress by AARO.", imageCredit:"U.S. Air Force MQ-9 sensor footage, released by AARO" },

  { id:"southasia", name:"South Asia MQ-9 Disk Object", date:"2023-01-23", lat:24.0, lon:68.0, location:"South Asia (specific country undisclosed)", precision:"approx", status:"explained", agency:"USAF (MQ-9 Reaper)", summary:"In January 2023, an MQ-9 Reaper drone operating in South Asia recorded footage of a disk-like object that appeared to hover near the aircraft's flight path, initially logged as an unidentified contact. AARO's director presented the case publicly alongside its resolution: analysis of the sensor geometry, range, and motion determined the object was a distant commuter aircraft, its apparent disk shape a product of viewing angle and sensor resolution at extreme range. The case was used in congressional briefings as a demonstration of AARO's resolution methodology and of how frequently apparent anomalies resolve to conventional aviation traffic once range is properly established.", source:"AARO director, Senate Armed Services briefing", image:"images/southasia.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from the MQ-9 sensor footage of the object, which AARO resolved as a distant commuter aircraft distorted by viewing angle and range.", imageCredit:"U.S. Air Force MQ-9 sensor footage, released by AARO" },

  { id:"yemenorb", name:"Yemen Coast Orb / Hellfire Engagement", date:"2024-10-30", lat:14.5, lon:49.0, location:"Off the coast of Yemen", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. military (MQ-9 drones)", summary:"On October 30, 2024, footage recorded by a U.S. MQ-9 drone off the Yemeni coast shows a second drone firing an AGM-114 Hellfire missile at an unidentified spherical object being tracked in flight; the missile appears to strike or pass extremely close to the object, which continues on its course apparently unaffected. The footage was released publicly by Rep. Eric Burlison at a House Oversight subcommittee hearing in September 2025 and covered by major networks. The engagement is notable as a rare documented instance of U.S. forces firing on an unidentified object. No official explanation for the object or confirmation of the engagement's outcome has been released.", source:"House Oversight UAP hearing, September 2025", image:"images/yemenorb.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from the MQ-9 drone footage shown at the 2025 House Oversight hearing, showing the tracked object during the missile engagement.", imageCredit:"U.S. military sensor footage, released at House Oversight subcommittee hearing" },

  { id:"langleyswarm", name:"Langley AFB Drone Swarm", date:"2023-12-06", lat:37.08, lon:-76.36, location:"Joint Base Langley-Eustis, Hampton Roads, Virginia", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF / FBI / AARO", summary:"Beginning December 6, 2023, groups of a dozen or more unidentified drones repeatedly overflew Joint Base Langley-Eustis after sunset on 17 consecutive nights, at times extending over Naval Station Norfolk. The incursions were significant enough that the Air Force temporarily repositioned F-22 fighters to another base. Observers described craft of varying sizes flying in loose formation at altitudes and speeds beyond typical hobbyist capability. Despite involvement from the Air Force, FBI, and AARO, and the deployment of counter-drone detection equipment, no operator was ever identified and no attribution was established. The episode became a central example in congressional discussion of gaps in domestic counter-drone authority.", source:"USAF/FBI statements; congressional testimony", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"langleytriangle", name:"Langley AFB Triangle", date:"2021", lat:37.08, lon:-76.36, location:"Langley Air Force Base, Virginia", precision:"exact", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Air Force", summary:"Air Force veteran Dylan Borland testified to Congress in 2025 about an encounter he reported at Langley Air Force Base in the summer of 2021, describing a large triangular craft hovering silently at low altitude with what he characterized as lava-like glowing plasma along its underside before it ascended rapidly out of sight. Borland stated he reported the sighting through his chain of command at the time. No sensor data, photographs, or corroborating official records for the encounter have been publicly released, and the account rests entirely on witness testimony. The reported location later became notable as the site of the separate, well-documented December 2023 drone incursions.", source:"D. Borland congressional testimony (2025)", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"njdrones", name:"New Jersey / Tri-State Drone Sightings", date:"2024-11", lat:40.22, lon:-74.52, location:"New Jersey (spreading to NY/PA), regional", precision:"approx", status:"explained", agency:"FBI / DHS", summary:"Beginning in November 2024, thousands of reports of large nighttime drones flying over New Jersey — and subsequently New York and Pennsylvania — prompted a federal response involving the FBI, DHS, FAA, and a dedicated task force, along with temporary flight restrictions over some sites. After investigation, federal officials concluded the overwhelming majority of sightings were authorized commercial drones, hobbyist aircraft, law enforcement and news helicopters, or misidentified manned aircraft and stars, and found no evidence of foreign involvement or public safety threat. A small number of reports near sensitive sites were not individually resolved. The episode is frequently cited as an example of how a reporting wave can amplify ordinary aviation activity into a perceived anomaly.", source:"FBI/DHS joint statements; congressional briefing", image:"images/njdrones.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A witness photograph of lights in the night sky taken in New Jersey during the December 2024 sighting wave. Genuine witness photo; federal investigators attributed the wave overwhelmingly to conventional aircraft and drones.", imageCredit:"Witness photograph, widely published in news coverage" },

  { id:"vabeachswarm", name:"Virginia Coast Navy UAP Swarm", date:"2025-05", lat:36.9, lon:-75.3, location:"Off the coast of Virginia, Atlantic Ocean", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"Documented in AARO's FY2025 Consolidated Annual Report, Navy assets operating off the Virginia coast reported roughly 100 airborne UAP alongside two likely uncrewed surface vessels — the only maritime-domain case among the 319 new reports AARO received that year, and one of the largest single-count encounters ever logged in an annual report. AARO stated it was actively investigating in coordination with the reporting unit. No imagery, sensor stills, or renderings have been publicly released for the case, and AARO has repeatedly cited the absence of timely, actionable sensor data as a recurring constraint on resolving reports of this kind.", source:"AARO FY2025 Consolidated Annual Report", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"motherorb", name:"Western U.S. “Orbs Launching Orbs” Event", date:"2023-10", lat:39.8, lon:-108.5, location:"Undisclosed sensitive site, western United States", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"Federal law-enforcement agents / FBI", summary:"In October 2023, federal agents near a sensitive national-security site in the western United States reported an orange “mother orb” that repeatedly released smaller red orbs, observed over two days by six agents. Because no camera or sensor captured the event, the FBI commissioned official digital renderings from witness testimony to preserve the reported geometry and behavior of the objects — ten such illustrations accompany the case file. The underlying report was signed by AARO Director Jon Kosloski and released as part of the PURSUE declassification effort in June 2026. The case remains formally unresolved.", source:"AARO Unresolved Case Analysis Update; PURSUE Release 03", image:"images/motherorb.jpg", imageType:"illustration", imageCaption:"Official FBI digital rendering (FBI-UAP-D023) recreating the witnesses' account: reddish orbs in a V-formation with paired white orbs over barren hills. An artist's recreation produced from testimony — not a photograph, as no camera captured the event.", imageCredit:"FBI-UAP-D023, U.S. Department of War, PURSUE Release 03 (June 2026)" },

  { id:"cospringspotato", name:"Cheyenne Mountain “Potato-Shaped” Object", date:"2022-02", lat:38.74, lon:-104.85, location:"Near Cheyenne Mountain / Fort Carson, Colorado Springs, CO", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. Army personnel / FBI", summary:"In February 2022, a former U.S. Army intelligence officer and four fellow unit members observed a motionless, potato-shaped object over Cheyenne Mountain on a clear morning as they left an office building near Fort Carson. Witnesses described it as creamy-white and opalescent, somewhat translucent with a slight shimmer, composed of irregular non-symmetrical panels resembling articulating fish scales that shifted in slow waves from multiple origin points — while the object itself remained perfectly still. The case was released as a three-file package including an FBI interview record, an official digital rendering, and an Intelligence Community analysis, which assessed with low confidence that sunlight backscattering off snow and low cloud might explain it. The case remains formally unresolved.", source:"PURSUE Release 03 (June 2026); FBI case file", image:"images/cospringspotato.jpg", imageType:"illustration", imageCaption:"Official FBI digital rendering (FBI-UAP-D003) depicting the object as described by the five witnesses. An artist's rendering produced from testimony — not a photograph.", imageCredit:"FBI-UAP-D003, U.S. Department of War, PURSUE Release 03 (June 2026)" },

  { id:"cospringstriangle", name:"Colorado Springs Triangle", date:"2023-10", lat:38.86, lon:-104.76, location:"Colorado Springs, CO area", precision:"approx", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"AARO / FBI", summary:"In October 2023, personnel near a military installation outside Colorado Springs reported a large, dark and reportedly translucent triangular object with a light at each of its three corners, hovering low against the night sky. The Department of War's fifth PURSUE tranche paired the account with an official FBI digital rendering rather than any sensor footage. The same release also declassified a visually similar case — a large triangular object reported over Bagram Air Base, Afghanistan in June 2002 — inviting comparison across the two decades separating them. Large triangular objects remain one of the most recurring shape categories across the PURSUE releases, alongside orbs.", source:"PURSUE Release 05 (Aug. 2026); FBI case file", image:"images/cospringstriangle.jpeg", imageType:"illustration", imageCaption:"Official FBI digital rendering (FBI-UAP-D031) of a black triangle with a red light at each corner, produced from witness description. An artist's rendering — not a photograph.", imageCredit:"FBI-UAP-D031, U.S. Department of War, PURSUE Release 05 (Aug. 2026)" },

  { id:"nevadadrones", name:"Nevada Nuclear Security Site Drone Incursions", date:"2023-10", lat:36.82, lon:-115.93, location:"Nevada National Security Site, near Las Vegas", precision:"exact", status:"unresolved", agency:"DOE / NNSA", summary:"Beginning in October 2023, personnel at the Department of Energy's Nevada National Security Site detected repeated drone incursions over a three-day span near nuclear-weapons-related facilities — four drones picked up by detection systems and a fifth observed directly by employees. The operators and origin were never identified, and officials stated at the time they did not know who was flying them or why. NNSA officials visited the site in December 2023 to review counter-drone detection upgrades. The incident is among a broader set of unresolved drone reports near U.S. nuclear infrastructure documented in AARO's annual reporting.", source:"DOE/NNSA incident reporting; AARO annual report", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"neorbs", name:"Northeastern U.S. Orb Sightings", date:"2024-10", lat:42.5, lon:-73.0, location:"Northeastern United States (regional)", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"AARO / FBI", summary:"A recurring cluster of orb sightings concentrated in one sparsely populated corner of the northeastern United States is documented across four authenticated eyewitness videos spanning November 2021 to July 2025, released together in the PURSUE declassification effort. In the July 2025 video, a witness recorded a brilliant red sphere roughly a meter across with a white plasma-like core, hovering approximately 25 feet off the ground, before an identical second orb joined it and the pair appeared to merge. A related October 2024 video recorded a similar sphere hovering above a pond for roughly 45 minutes. The file package also includes a report in which two FBI special agents themselves witnessed a UAP during a November 2024 site survey; the Bureau assessed the civilian witnesses as highly credible. All four videos remain formally unresolved.", source:"FBI case file corpus; PURSUE Release 03", image:"images/neorbs.jpg", imageType:"video-still", imageCaption:"Frame from officially released FBI video (FBI-UAP-PR004) showing a red orb with a glowing white core, recorded by an eyewitness in July 2025.", imageCredit:"FBI-UAP-PR004, released via DVIDS, U.S. Department of War PURSUE Release 03" },

  { id:"dccook", name:"D.C. Cook Nuclear Plant Crashed UAS", date:"2023-08-03", lat:41.97, lon:-86.56, location:"Bridgman, Michigan", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"Plant security / local law enforcement", summary:"On August 3, 2023, security personnel at the D.C. Cook Nuclear Power Plant recovered a crashed unmanned aircraft system on plant property and turned it over to Berrien County law enforcement for investigation. The incident is documented in AARO's FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report, which covered 757 total UAP reports for the period, of which AARO resolved 118 — all attributed to conventional objects. The case is categorized as resolved: a physical drone was recovered, making it one of the few nuclear-site incidents in the reporting period with a recovered object rather than only observational data. The operator's identity was not disclosed in the public reporting.", source:"AARO FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"fewarren", name:"Nuclear Missile Field Drone Incursions", date:"2023-12", lat:41.16, lon:-104.87, location:"F.E. Warren AFB region, Wyoming", precision:"approx", status:"uncorroborated", agency:"U.S. Air Force", summary:"In December 2023, formations of drones were reported flying repeatedly over the ICBM silo fields surrounding F.E. Warren Air Force Base near Cheyenne, part of a broader wave of unexplained drone activity over sensitive U.S. military installations that same month — including the concurrent multi-night incursions at Langley AFB in Virginia. AARO logged 18 total reports of drone activity near U.S. nuclear infrastructure, weapons, and launch sites during the fiscal year. F.E. Warren has since maintained counter-drone detection systems over its missile fields. No operator was identified and no official imagery specific to the silo-field incursions has been released.", source:"AARO FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"canaveralstarlink", name:"Cape Canaveral Starlink Misidentification", date:"2023-2024", lat:28.39, lon:-80.61, location:"Cape Canaveral, Florida", precision:"exact", status:"explained", agency:"Commercial airline pilot / AARO", summary:"A commercial pilot flying near Cape Canaveral reported a line of flashing lights in the night sky, logged in AARO's FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report. Analysts cross-referenced the sighting's time and location against SpaceX's launch manifest and found that a Falcon 9 carrying Starlink satellites had lifted off roughly an hour earlier, placing a “satellite train” — dozens of newly deployed satellites still flying in tight, evenly spaced formation before dispersing to operational orbit — along the pilot's flight path. AARO cited the case as representative of how rapidly growing satellite constellations are generating explained outcomes for sightings that would previously have remained unresolved. The case raised no flight-safety concerns.", source:"AARO FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report", image:"images/canaveralstarlink.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A Starlink “satellite train” photographed shortly after a launch — illustrative of the phenomenon AARO determined the pilot had seen. This is a photo of the phenomenon type, not of the actual reported sighting.", imageCredit:"Steve Elliott, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0)" },

  { id:"nycylinder", name:"Atlantic Cylindrical Object Near-Miss", date:"2023-2024", lat:40.5, lon:-72.0, location:"Off the New York coast, Atlantic Ocean", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"Commercial airline crew / FAA", summary:"Documented in AARO's FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report, a commercial aircrew reported a near-miss with a cylindrical object while over the Atlantic off the New York coast. Of 757 total UAP reports received in the period — including 392 relayed via the FAA — this was the only one AARO flagged as raising a possible flight-safety issue. The office has not publicly disclosed the airline, precise date, or altitude, and stated the case remained under continuing investigation. No photograph, radar image, or video has been released. Researchers have cited the case as illustrative of how rarely AARO's dataset contains safety-relevant encounters despite hundreds of annual reports.", source:"AARO FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"vafuelcycle", name:"Virginia Fuel Cycle Facility Sightings", date:"2023-2024", lat:37.5, lon:-77.0, location:"Nuclear fuel cycle facility, Virginia (regional)", precision:"approx", status:"explained", agency:"DoD / DOE facility security", summary:"Between October 10 and 15, 2023, personnel at a nuclear fuel cycle facility in Lynchburg, Virginia — a site that manufactures naval nuclear reactor fuel — reported unmanned aircraft system flyovers on six consecutive nights, with a single drone observed on each occasion. The incident was among 18 reports AARO received during the FY2024 period from the Administrator for Nuclear Security and the Chairman of the Nuclear Regulatory Commission concerning activity near U.S. nuclear infrastructure. AARO categorized all 18 of these nuclear-site incidents as conventional drone activity rather than anomalous phenomena. Flight duration and operator identity were not established, and no imagery was publicly released.", source:"AARO FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"satflare", name:"Satellite Flaring Misidentification Wave", date:"2024-2025", lat:39.5, lon:-98.35, location:"United States (nationwide)", precision:"approx", status:"explained", agency:"AARO analytics", summary:"In its FY2025 Consolidated Annual Report, AARO disclosed that new 3D-modeling and simulation capabilities allowed analysts to systematically attribute 238 previously separate UAP reports nationwide to satellite flaring — sunlight glinting off satellites in low Earth orbit, which can appear as a sudden bright flash or moving point of light. With more than 6,700 Starlink satellites alone in orbit, this misidentification source has become increasingly common, and AARO released a dedicated information paper and explanatory video on the phenomenon in April 2025. The modeling capability was significant because flare events depend on precise satellite position, solar angle, and observer location — variables that had previously made case-by-case confirmation impractical.", source:"AARO FY2025 Consolidated Annual Report", image:"images/satflare.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A long-exposure composite of a Starlink satellite overpass — illustrative of the sunlight-reflection events AARO's modeling attributed 238 reports to. A photo of the phenomenon type, not of any specific reported sighting.", imageCredit:"Mike Lewinski, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0)" },

  { id:"heliorb", name:"Military Helicopter Orb Encounter", date:"2025", lat:39.0, lon:-105.0, location:"Location undisclosed, western United States (regional)", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"U.S. military helicopter aircrew", summary:"A written first-person narrative by a serving senior U.S. intelligence officer, declassified and released in May 2026 as part of the PURSUE effort, describes an encounter aboard a military helicopter in late 2025. The officer, flying with a colleague and two pilots, departed a Joint Operations Center to investigate loud thuds and prior nights' sightings reported on a military test range. Over roughly an hour, the crew observed two large stationary orange-and-white oval orbs near the rotor disk, followed by a swarm of smaller orbs forming a triangular arrangement, with sightings recurring in cycles of about 30 minutes; when fighter jets were scrambled, the orbs allegedly matched their speed and trajectory rather than fleeing. The exact location remains undisclosed and no imagery accompanies the account.", source:"Declassified narrative, PURSUE release (May 2026)", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"shadowed", name:"“Trailed or Shadowed” Pilot Encounters", date:"2023-2024", lat:38.0, lon:-95.0, location:"Locations undisclosed, U.S. military airspace (regional)", precision:"approx", status:"unresolved", agency:"USAF / Navy aircrews", summary:"In its FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report, AARO singled out 21 of the 757 reports received during the May 2023–June 2024 period for deeper follow-on study based on reported anomalous characteristics or behaviors. Three of those cases involved U.S. military or commercial aircrews describing being trailed or shadowed by a UAP during flight — a category distinct from the report's flight-safety and near-miss cases. AARO stated it had no indication or confirming intelligence that these incidents were attributable to a foreign adversary's collection activity, though they remained formally unresolved. The 21 flagged cases were being jointly examined by AARO's technical staff, intelligence community partners, and outside science-and-technology collaborators.", source:"AARO FY2024 Consolidated Annual Report", image:null, imageType:null, imageCaption:null, imageCredit:null },

  { id:"radarmalfunction", name:"East Coast Navy Radar-Malfunction Encounter", date:"2023", lat:35.0, lon:-73.0, location:"Atlantic Coast warning area (regional)", precision:"approx", status:"explained", agency:"U.S. Navy", summary:"During a training exercise in January 2023, a Navy pilot's onboard radar detected objects flying between 16,000 and 18,000 feet; as the aircraft closed to within roughly 4,000 feet of one object, its radar system malfunctioned and remained inoperative for the rest of the mission. A post-flight inspection found a tripped circuit breaker, but AARO's investigation found no causal link between the object and the electrical fault. Reconstructing the flight geometry and solar angle, AARO assessed with moderate confidence that the object was a large lighter-than-air platform such as a commercial lighting or advertising balloon. No photograph of the object was captured; the identification was inferred from reconstructed flight data.", source:"AARO case-resolution reporting", image:"images/radarmalfunction.jpg", imageType:"photo", imageCaption:"A large super-pressure research balloon aloft — illustrative of the lighter-than-air object class AARO concluded was responsible. A photo of the object type, not the actual object involved, which was never photographed.", imageCredit:"NASA/Bill Rodman, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain)" }
];
